%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 80a1f2cfa0ad20647bab6ee8a4a9b4f8, type: 3}
  m_Name: FinalShortVirusLecture
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  slides:
  - title: Welcome
    content: Welcome To The Biology Lecture
    slideImage: {fileID: 0}
  - title: SLide1:Openning
    content: Hello everyone! Today, we will look at how the immune system detects,
      responds to, and remembers threats.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6558a0ecc8ec5ec47884211ba066a44e, type: 3}
  - title: Slide2:Mosquito
    content: "A mosquito lands on your arm, injects its chemicals into your skin,
      and begins to feed.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f70914385122c544bacf46d54909df9e, type: 3}
  - title: Slide2:Mosquito
    content: " would not even know it was there, if not for the red lump that appears,
      accompanied by a telltale itch.\r\nIt is a nuisance, but that bump is also
      an important signal.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f70914385122c544bacf46d54909df9e, type: 3}
  - title: Slide2:Mosquito
    content: "\rIt shows that you are protected by your immune system, your body\u2019s
      major safeguard against infection, illness, and disease."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f70914385122c544bacf46d54909df9e, type: 3}
  - title: Slides3:Threats
    content: "This system is a vast network of cells, tissues, and organs that coordinate
      your body\u2019s defenses against any threats to your health.\r\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 318c6d3b32e5b6d4c9f15bda17a7fb9d, type: 3}
  - title: Slides3:Threats
    content: "This system is a vast network of cells, tissues, and organs that coordinate
      your body\u2019s defenses against any threats to your health.\r\nWithout it,
      you would be exposed to billions of bacteria, viruses, and toxins that could
      make something as minor as a paper cut or a seasonal cold fatal.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 318c6d3b32e5b6d4c9f15bda17a7fb9d, type: 3}
  - title: Slide4:Immune System
    content: "The immune system relies on millions of defensive white blood cells,
      also known as leukocytes, that originate in our bone marrow.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c6d68dd52bb51104bade09948004c498, type: 3}
  - title: Slide4:Immune System
    content: "\rThese cells migrate into the bloodstream and the lymphatic system,
      a network of vessels that helps clear bodily toxins and waste.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c6d68dd52bb51104bade09948004c498, type: 3}
  - title: Slide4:Immune System
    content: "Our bodies are teeming with leukocytes.\r\n There are between 4,000
      and 11,000 in every microliter of blood."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c6d68dd52bb51104bade09948004c498, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide5: Antigens'
    content: "As they move around, leukocytes work like security personnel, constantly
      screening the blood, tissues, and organs for suspicious signs.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0d81d1d2b9ba24749a61a580e8ee0488, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide5: Antigens'
    content: "\rThis system mainly relies on cues called antigens.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0d81d1d2b9ba24749a61a580e8ee0488, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide5: Antigens'
    content: "\r\rAntigens are molecular traces found on the surface of pathogens
      and other foreign substances, and they betray the presence of invaders."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0d81d1d2b9ba24749a61a580e8ee0488, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide6: Detect Signs'
    content: "As soon as leukocytes detect these signs, it takes only minutes for
      the body\u2019s protective immune response to begin.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e841b9a6e1f2ed544b327bb088d09a47, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide6: Detect Signs'
    content: "\rThreats to our bodies are hugely variable, so the immune response
      has to be equally adaptable."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e841b9a6e1f2ed544b327bb088d09a47, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide6: Detect Signs'
    content: "\rThat means relying on many different types of leukocytes to tackle
      threats in different ways."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e841b9a6e1f2ed544b327bb088d09a47, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide7: Leukocytes'
    content: "Despite this diversity, leukocytes can be classified into two main
      cellular groups, and these groups coordinate a two-pronged attack.\r\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 70aefb1bd7560a3428242fe79a96b02c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide7: Leukocytes'
    content: "\rFirst, phagocytes help trigger the immune response by sending macrophages
      and dendritic cells into the blood.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 70aefb1bd7560a3428242fe79a96b02c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide8: Phagocytes'
    content: "As these cells circulate, they destroy any foreign cells they encounter
      simply by consuming them.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7a98a591490ec4d488394c8e4dc03d6c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide8: Phagocytes'
    content: "\rThat process also allows phagocytes to identify the antigens on the
      invaders they have just ingested."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7a98a591490ec4d488394c8e4dc03d6c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide8: Phagocytes'
    content: "\rThey then transmit this information to the second major cell group
      that orchestrates the defense: the lymphocytes."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7a98a591490ec4d488394c8e4dc03d6c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide9: Lymphocytes'
    content: A group of lymphocytes called T cells goes in search of infected body
      cells and swiftly kills them off.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f87eb916c1996f045b3a1c09f257cbd5, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide9: Lymphocytes'
    content: 'Meanwhile, B cells and helper T cells use the information gathered
      from those unique antigens to begin producing special proteins called antibodies.

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f87eb916c1996f045b3a1c09f257cbd5, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide10: Antibody'
    content: "This is the pi\xE8ce de r\xE9sistance, the key step in the response.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d9a348f8a480c084caf7651e63bd1820, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide10: Antibody'
    content: "\rEach antigen has a unique matching antibody that can latch onto it
      like a lock and key and help destroy the invading cells.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d9a348f8a480c084caf7651e63bd1820, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide10: Antibody'
    content: "\rB cells can produce millions of these antibodies.\r\nThey then cycle
      through the body and attack the invaders until the worst of the threat has
      been neutralized."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d9a348f8a480c084caf7651e63bd1820, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: While all of this is going on, familiar symptoms, such as high temperatures
      and swelling, are actually processes designed to aid the immune response.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: 'A warmer body makes it harder for bacteria and viruses to reproduce
      and spread because many of them are temperature-sensitive.

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: And when body cells are damaged, they release chemicals that make fluid
      leak into the surrounding tissues, causing swelling.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: 'That swelling also attracts phagocytes, which then consume both the
      invaders and the damaged cells.

      Usually, an immune response will eradicate
      a threat within a few days.

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: It will not always stop you from getting ill, but that is not its only
      purpose.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: Its actual job is to stop a threat from escalating to dangerous levels
      inside your body.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide12: B-T-Cells'
    content: "And through constant surveillance over time, the immune system provides
      another major benefit: it helps us develop long-term immunity.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6b28fdccf1d165a45a1d7333f6c9e7fe, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide12: B-T-Cells'
    content: "\rWhen B cells and T cells identify antigens, they can use that information
      to recognize the same invaders in the future.\r\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6b28fdccf1d165a45a1d7333f6c9e7fe, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide12: B-T-Cells'
    content: "\rSo when a threat revisits, those cells can swiftly deploy the right
      antibodies to tackle it before it affects many more cells.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6b28fdccf1d165a45a1d7333f6c9e7fe, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide12: B-T-Cells'
    content: "\r\rThat is how you can develop immunity to certain diseases, like
      chickenpox.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6b28fdccf1d165a45a1d7333f6c9e7fe, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide13: Healthy Cells'
    content: "It does not always work perfectly.\r\nSome people have autoimmune diseases,
      which trick the immune system into attacking the body\u2019s own perfectly
      healthy cells.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f155c8220c26e354d89771a866ccc647, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide13: Healthy Cells'
    content: "\rNo one knows exactly what causes these conditions, but they sabotage
      the immune system to varying degrees and underlie disorders such as arthritis,
      type 1 diabetes, and multiple sclerosis.\r\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f155c8220c26e354d89771a866ccc647, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide13: Healthy Cells'
    content: "\r\rFor most individuals, however, a healthy immune system will successfully
      fight off an estimated 300 colds and innumerable other potential infections
      over the course of a lifetime.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f155c8220c26e354d89771a866ccc647, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide13: Healthy Cells'
    content: "\rWithout it, those threats would escalate into something far more
      dangerous.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f155c8220c26e354d89771a866ccc647, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide14: Conclusion'
    content: 'So the next time you catch a cold or scratch a mosquito bite, think
      of the immune system.

      

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3db5aa5ce6b74284bb199ec9f328a776, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide14: Conclusion'
    content: ".\rIt is working constantly to protect you.\r\nWe owe it our lives.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3db5aa5ce6b74284bb199ec9f328a776, type: 3}
